const faq = [
  { q: 'Do wolves benefit from ravens?', a: 'The benefit to wolves is less clear than the benefit to ravens. Some researchers (Heinrich, Marzluff) have proposed that ravens alert wolves to prey opportunities via vocalisations, but this has not been confirmed experimentally. Wolves are known to tolerate ravens and occasionally play with them, but the idea that wolves deliberately partner with ravens is not supported by current evidence. The relationship is best described as commensal (ravens benefit; effect on wolves is neutral or mildly positive) rather than mutualistic.' },
  { q: 'How many ravens follow a wolf pack?', a: 'Field studies at Yellowstone and in Scandinavia record from a handful to 30–40+ ravens gathering at large ungulate kills by wolf packs. At very large carcasses (moose, bison) in winter, numbers can be higher. The ravens are not permanently attached to a specific pack — they roam widely and converge on carcasses from large areas.' },
  { q: 'Is this relationship only in the Northern Hemisphere?', a: 'The Common Raven–wolf relationship is best documented in boreal and arctic ecosystems of North America and northern Europe (Scandinavia, Iceland). Both Common Raven and Grey Wolf have overlapping ranges across the Northern Hemisphere. Similar relationships between African ravens (White-necked Raven, Brown-necked Raven) and large African carnivores have been noted but are less systematically studied.' },
];
